Pub/Sub

1. What is Pub/Sub?
	Publish-Subscribe is a messaging pattern where a publisher sends an event to a topic, and multiple independent subscribers receive their own copy.
	                 ┌── Inventory
		         │
	Publisher → Topic ─── Notifications
		         │
		         ├── Analytics
		         │
		         └── Fraud Detection
	The publisher doesn't need to know who the subscribers are.
	main benefit - Decoupling + asynchronous processing + one-to-many communication.
	
2. Real-World Example
	When an order is placed:
		Order Service
		     ↓
		 OrderPlaced
		     ↓
		   Topic
		 ┌───┼────┬──────┐
		 ↓   ↓    ↓      ↓
		Inventory Email Analytics Fraud
	
	The Order Service doesn't directly call four services.
	New consumers can be added without modifying the publisher. That's the whole point of avoiding a giant chain of service-to-service calls.
	
3. Core Components
	| Component        | Purpose                               |
	| ---------------- | ------------------------------------- |
	| **Publisher**    | Creates and publishes events          |
	| **Topic**        | Named stream/channel receiving events |
	| **Subscription** | Connects a consumer to a topic        |
	| **Message**      | Contains event information            |
	| **Subscriber**   | Consumes and processes events         |

	Topic = where events are published
	Subscription = a subscriber's own delivery state
	
4. Pub/Sub vs Queue
	|                     | Work Queue      | Pub/Sub                       |
	| ------------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------- |
	| Message received by | One worker      | Every interested subscription |
	| Purpose             | Distribute work | Broadcast an event            |
	| Example             | Resize image    | User registered               |
	| Model               | One-to-one      | One-to-many                   |

	Queue: "Someone needs to do this."
	Pub/Sub: "This happened. Whoever cares, react to it."
	
5. Fan-Out
	Fan-out = one event → multiple subscriptions.
	          OrderPlaced
		        	↓
		     Topic
		┌──────┼──────┐
		↓    	  ↓      	↓
	    Inventory Email Analytics
	Why useful? You can add another subscriber without changing the publisher.
	Before: Order → Inventory + Email
	Later: Order → Inventory + Email + Fraud + Recommendations
	The publisher remains unchanged.
	
6. Message Filtering
	A subscription can receive only messages matching certain criteria.
	e.g - 
		Topic: payment.events

			 ┌── ledger-sub
			 │   payment=SUCCESS
			 │
		Events ──┼── fraud-sub
			 │   payment=FAILED
			 │
			 └── analytics-sub
			     all payments
	Prefer broker-level filtering when available rather than making every consumer receive and discard irrelevant messages.
	
7. Push vs Pull
	Push - Broker pushes messages to subscriber. It has low latency, simple for web endpoints. Subscriber must handle bursts and endpoint must remain reachable.
	Pull - Subscriber asks broker for messages. Consumer controls rate, Better control over batching/workers. Requires polling workers.
	
8. Consumer Groups / Shared Subscription
	Fan-out and load balancing are different.
	multiple subscriptions -> fan out
	multiple workers same subscription -> load balancing.
	
9. Durability & Replay
	Pub/Sub systems can have different message retention models.
		Temporary - Subscriber offline -> misses message. Good for low-value live updates.
		Durable - Subscriber offline -> Message waits -> Subscriber returns -> Processes message. Good for business events/background processing.
		Replayable - Messages are retained and consumers can go back and process older events. useful for Reprocessing, Data pipelines, Stream processing, Recovery.
		
10. Event Design
	A good event should contain enough information for safe processing.
	e.g - 
		{
		  "eventId": "evt_123",
		  "eventType": "OrderCreated",
		  "orderId": "order_456",
		  "timestamp": "...",
		  "version": 1,
		  "traceId": "...",
		  "data": {}
		}
	Important fields:
		Event ID
		Event type
		Entity ID
		Timestamp
		Version
		Event data
		Trace/correlation ID
	The publisher should announce what happened, while subscribers decide what to do.
	
11. Event Evolution
	Events become contracts once multiple services consume them.
	prefer - add new field -> keep old fields -> Old consumers continue working
	avoid casually - Rename/remove existing field -> Consumer breaks.
	Good practices:
		Prefer backward-compatible changes
		Add fields rather than removing/changing them
		Use event versions when necessary
		Consider schema registries for large systems
		Don't publish raw DB rows as your event contract
		
12. Common Patterns
	Event Notification
	Fan-Out → Queues
	Event Carries State
	Pub/Sub ≠ Event Sourcing, Event sourcing uses an append-only event log as the source of truth.
	
13. Popular implementations.
	Kafka, Amazon SNS, Google Cloud Pub/Sub, Azure service Bus Topics, Redis Pub/Sub.
	
14. When to Use Pub/Sub
	Use when
		Multiple services react to the same event
		Publisher shouldn't know consumers
		Processing can happen asynchronously
		Eventual consistency is acceptable
		New consumers may be added later
	Avoid when
		Caller needs an immediate response
		Only one handler exists
		Workflow requires strict step-by-step control
		Consumers cannot tolerate duplicates/out-of-order events
		Nobody is monitoring subscriptions/backlogs
		
