Change Data Capture (CDC)

1. What is CDC?
	capture database changes as they happen and send them to other systems.
	Instead of repeatedly polling the database: DB → query every 5 min → find changes → copy
	Application -> DB -> CDC -> Event Stream -> Search, Cache, Warehouse, Analytics.
	
2. What Does CDC Capture?
	Usually:
		INSERT
		UPDATE
		DELETE
	A CDC event may contain:
		| Field            | Purpose              |
		| ---------------- | -------------------- |
		| Operation        | Insert/update/delete |
		| Table            | Source table         |
		| Primary Key      | Identifies row       |
		| Before           | Previous value       |
		| After            | New value            |
		| Commit position  | Position in DB log   |
		| Timestamp        | Commit time          |
		| Transaction info | Ordering/context     |

3. CDC Pipeline
	Database
	   ↓
	Capture changes
	   ↓
	Build CDC event
	   ↓
	Publish
	   ↓
	Kafka / Queue / Stream
	   ↓
	Consumers
	   ├── Search
	   ├── Cache
	   ├── Warehouse
	   └── Analytics
	
	Four basic steps - Capture → Build event → Publish → Consume
	
4. CDC Implementation Approaches
	A. Timestamp-Based CDC
		use something like - updated_at then periodically query where updated_at > last_sync_time;
		pros - very simple, No special infrastructure needs
		Cons - Hard deletes can be missed, Immediately updates can be lost, Polling ads DB load
	B. Trigger-Based CDC
		Database triggers write changes into an audit/outbox table.
		pros - Can capture before/after values
		cons - add small work to normal DB writes. Trigger maintenance is painful.
	Use: when transaction-log access isn't available or when an outbox/audit table is appropriate.
	
5. Log-Based CDC
	Reads the database's transaction log.
	e.g 
		MySQL       → Binlog
		PostgreSQL  → WAL / Logical Replication
		MongoDB     → Oplog / Change Streams
	Application -> DB -> Transaction log -> CDC Connector -> Kafka/stream -> Consumer.
	pros - good ordering, scales well, low impact on application writes, can resume from saved log postions
	cons - more setup, db-specific configuration, need sufficient log retention, schema evolution needs planning.
	Log-based CDC is usually the preferred approach for high-volume production systems.
	
6. Transactional Outbox + CDC
	One of the most important patterns.
	problem - 
		DB write ✅
		Kafka publish ❌
	Now DB and event stream disagree.
	Outbox solution - Write both in one DB transaction:
				Transaction
					 ├── Update business table
					 └── Insert event into outbox
						  ↓
					       COMMIT
						  ↓
					       CDC reads outbox
						  ↓
						Kafka
						  ↓
					      Consumers
	If the transaction rolls back:
		Business update ❌
		Outbox event ❌
	Outbox solves the dual-write problem.
	
7. Common use cases -
	Search Indexing - DB → CDC → Elasticsearch/OpenSearch
	Data Warehouse - DB → CDC → Data Lake/Warehouse
	Cache Invalidation - 
	Read Models - A service can consume changes and build its own optimized read model.
	Audit - Capture changes for history/debugging/compliance.

8. Operational Problems
	Initial Snapshot - Existing data must be copied first. Large tables can make snapshots expensive.
	CDC Lag - monitor Connector Lag, Oldest unprocessed event age, Consumer lag, DLQ messages
	Log Retention - If CDC is down too long and the database deletes the required transaction logs and it cannot resume normally so need snapshot/recovery.So log retention must cover expected outages.
	Ordering - Source DB may provide commit ordering, but downstream partitioning and parallel consumers can change processing order. use - version numbers, commit positions, timestamps.
	Deletes - Deletes need explicit handling: hard delete? , soft delete?, Keep for audit?, Tombstone.
	Schema Evolution - changing or removing column can break downstream consumers. Prefer - Add new optional field, keep old fields temporarily, migrate consumers.
	
CDC turns committed database changes into a stream that other systems can consume, keeping them synchronized without repeatedly polling the source database.
